From 1495126138e086080f0163bee27fafbdf956a1d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Moritz Bunkus <mo@bunkus.online>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 13:01:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] avilib: guard against heap overflow due to integer
 multiplication wrapping
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`wLongsPerEntry` is an `uint16_t`, `nEntriesInUse` is an
`uint32_t`. Automatic integer promotion during multiplication means
`wLongsPerEntry` gets promoted to `uint32_t`, then those values are
multiplied. If the result is bigger than `max(uint32_t)` it'll wrap
around (colloquially: overflow).

As the result is used for allocating memory and subsequent code
assumes all required space has been allocated, it'll try to read
from/write to memory beyond the end of the allocated buffer → heap
overflow/invalid memory access.

Affects the ODML index handling.
---
 lib/avilib-0.6.10/avilib.c | 38 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----------
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/lib/avilib-0.6.10/avilib.c b/lib/avilib-0.6.10/avilib.c
index 560cf4522..85a284fc1 100644
--- a/lib/avilib-0.6.10/avilib.c
+++ b/lib/avilib-0.6.10/avilib.c
@@ -61,6 +61,18 @@ static void *_saferealloc(void *p, size_t size, int line) {
   return mem;
 }
 
+#define safemulu(a, b) _safemulu(a, b, __LINE__)
+static size_t _safemulu(size_t a, size_t b, int line) {
